5. Memory Protection Using Base and Limit Registers
Aim

To implement a program that provides memory protection using base and limit registers and prevents unauthorized memory access.

Algorithm
Read the base address.
Read the limit value.
Calculate the valid memory range:

Base ≤ Physical Address < Base + Limit

Read the logical address.
Check whether:

0 ≤ Logical Address < Limit

If valid, calculate the physical address.
Otherwise, deny access.
C Program
#include <stdio.h>

int main() {
    int base, limit;
    int logicalAddress, physicalAddress;

    printf("Enter Base Address: ");
    scanf("%d", &base);

    printf("Enter Limit: ");
    scanf("%d", &limit);

    printf("\nValid Logical Address Range: 0 to %d\n",
           limit - 1);

    printf("Enter Logical Address: ");
    scanf("%d", &logicalAddress);

    if (logicalAddress >= 0 && logicalAddress < limit) {

        physicalAddress = base + logicalAddress;

        printf("\nAccess Granted.\n");
        printf("Logical Address : %d\n", logicalAddress);
        printf("Physical Address: %d\n", physicalAddress);

    } else {

        printf("\nAccess Denied!\n");
        printf("Unauthorized memory access detected.\n");
    }

    return 0;
}

